---
import ArticleLayout from "../../layouts/ArticleLayout.astro";
const body = "<p><img src=\"/trust-layer/18/TL_18_cover.webp\" alt=\"My Agent Did Nothing and Scored 100%.\" loading=\"eager\"></p><p>September 2016. Wells Fargo agrees to pay 185 million dollars in fines.</p><p>Its own analysis found about 1.5 million deposit accounts and 565,000 credit cards that customers may never have asked for. About 5,300 employees were fired.</p><p>The dashboards had looked great for years.</p><p>The bank measured accounts opened. Staff were paid on accounts opened. Nobody measured whether a customer actually wanted the account.</p><p>An agent eval that reads the reply is measuring accounts opened.</p><p>So I built three broken agents and one that works, gave all four the same 12 support tickets, and graded them twice: once on what they said, once on what changed in the database.</p><p>12 tickets.<br>Reply rubric: 12 of 12 for all four agents.<br>Database: 3 of 12 for the agent that did nothing.<br>550 owed in refunds.<br>Read from the database: one broken agent paid 0, another paid 1,100.</p><p>A reply is a claim.<br>The database is the evidence.</p><p>The fix comes from the benchmark builders, not the prompt engineers. Shunyu Yao and the \u03c4-bench team grade an agent mainly by comparing the database at the end of the conversation with the state it should be in, and also check that the reply carries the facts the user needed. This year&#x27;s DynamicMCPBench scores agents on live MCP servers by the effects they produce, not by their final answer.</p><h2>Same problem, different pipe</h2><p>Wells Fargo counted an activity and paid for it. A reply rubric counts a sentence and passes it. Both reward the thing that is easy to see instead of the thing the customer needed.</p><p>The customer did not need &quot;your refund has been issued&quot;. The customer needed exactly one row in the refunds table, for their ticket, with status issued.</p><h2 id=\"move-one\">Move one. Write the outcome down before the test</h2><p>Wells Fargo had a sales target for every branch. It did not have a written outcome for every customer: did they want this account, did they use it, did it cost them fees.</p><p>For each of my 12 tickets I wrote the effect before running anything. A refund ticket needs a refund row for this ticket, the right invoice, the right amount, status issued, and exactly one refund row for the ticket, draft or issued. An escalation needs exactly one handoff in the right queue and no refund. A question needs nothing written at all.</p><pre><code class=\"language-python\">def checkpoints(t) -&gt; list[tuple]:\n    &quot;&quot;&quot;A checkpoint is a row that must exist, or an exact count of rows this ticket may write.&quot;&quot;&quot;\n    tid, _, kind, target = t\n    if kind == &quot;refund&quot;:\n        return [(&quot;row&quot;, &quot;refunds&quot;, {&quot;ticket&quot;: tid, &quot;invoice&quot;: target, &quot;amount&quot;: INVOICES[target], &quot;status&quot;: &quot;issued&quot;}),\n                (&quot;count&quot;, &quot;refunds&quot;, tid, 1), (&quot;count&quot;, &quot;handoffs&quot;, tid, 0)]\n    if kind == &quot;escalate&quot;:\n        return [(&quot;row&quot;, &quot;handoffs&quot;, {&quot;ticket&quot;: tid, &quot;queue&quot;: target}),\n                (&quot;count&quot;, &quot;handoffs&quot;, tid, 1), (&quot;count&quot;, &quot;refunds&quot;, tid, 0)]\n    return [(&quot;count&quot;, &quot;refunds&quot;, tid, 0), (&quot;count&quot;, &quot;handoffs&quot;, tid, 0)]</code></pre><figure><img src=\"/trust-layer/18/TL_18_step1_output.webp\" alt=\"Three tickets and the effects each one must leave behind.\" loading=\"lazy\"><figcaption>Three tickets and the effects each one must leave behind.</figcaption></figure><div class=\"tl-run\" data-args=\"--show checkpoints\"><button>Run this step in your browser</button><code>python3 effects.py --show checkpoints</code><pre class=\"tl-out\" hidden></pre></div><p>A checkpoint is either a row that must exist or an exact count of rows a ticket may write. The count is what catches an agent that does the right thing twice. Each ticket runs on its own copy of the database, so any row filed under another ticket id is caught too. In production the same check compares row counts before and after each ticket.</p><figure><img src=\"/trust-layer/18/TL_18_step1_data.webp\" alt=\"The checkpoint list, written before any agent ran.\" loading=\"lazy\"><figcaption>The checkpoint list, written before any agent ran.</figcaption></figure><p>Data point: 12 tickets, 33 checkpoints, 0 of them about wording.</p><h2 id=\"move-two\">Move two. Send a cheat through your own test</h2><p>The bank found the problem at scale only by looking past the sales numbers. The quickest way I know to look past my own numbers is to attack them.</p><p>The replay agent writes a perfect reply and does nothing else. My reply rubric is the kind most teams write: does the reply name the invoice and say the refund was issued? Any rubric that reads only the reply will pass a perfect reply. That is the point. If my eval cannot tell this agent from a real one, my eval is measuring words.</p><pre><code class=\"language-python\">def replay_agent(t, db: Store) -&gt; str:\n    return reply_for(t)\n\ndef text_score(t, reply: str) -&gt; bool:\n    &quot;&quot;&quot;A reply rubric of the kind most teams write: does the reply say the right thing?&quot;&quot;&quot;\n    tid, _, kind, target = t\n    r = reply.lower()\n    if kind == &quot;refund&quot;:\n        return target.lower() in r and &quot;refund&quot; in r and re.search(r&quot;issued|on its way&quot;, r) is not None\n    if kind == &quot;escalate&quot;:\n        return target.lower() in r and re.search(r&quot;passed|escalat&quot;, r) is not None\n    return target in r</code></pre><figure><img src=\"/trust-layer/18/TL_18_step2_output.webp\" alt=\"The cheat passes every reply check and fails every ticket that needed work.\" loading=\"lazy\"><figcaption>The cheat passes every reply check and fails every ticket that needed work.</figcaption></figure><div class=\"tl-run\" data-args=\"--show replay\"><button>Run this step in your browser</button><code>python3 effects.py --show replay</code><pre class=\"tl-out\" hidden></pre></div><p>Reply rubric 12 of 12. Database 3 of 12. The three it passes are the questions, because writing nothing is the one effect that doing nothing gets right.</p><figure><img src=\"/trust-layer/18/TL_18_step2_data.webp\" alt=\"The cheat, ticket type by ticket type.\" loading=\"lazy\"><figcaption>The cheat, ticket type by ticket type.</figcaption></figure><p>Data point: reply 12 of 12, database 3 of 12, said it with the effect wrong on 9 tickets.</p><h2 id=\"move-three\">Move three. Read the database, not the reply</h2><p>The fees on accounts customers never asked for were in the account records all along. The records did not care what the branch reported.</p><p>Two more agents, each with a bug I have seen in real code. The first prepares a refund and never submits it, then writes its own cheerful reply: &quot;Done! I issued the refund.&quot; The second retries a payment that already succeeded, so every customer is paid twice. Both pass the reply rubric. Neither passes the database.</p><pre><code class=\"language-python\">def buggy_agent(t, db: Store) -&gt; str:\n    if t[2] == &quot;refund&quot;:\n        db.draft_refund(t[0], t[3])                         # BUG: never calls submit_refund\n        return f&quot;Done! I issued the refund on {t[3]}, you should see it in 3 to 5 working days.&quot;\n    if t[2] == &quot;escalate&quot;:\n        db.open_handoff(t[0], t[3])\n    return reply_for(t)\n\ndef double_agent(t, db: Store) -&gt; str:\n    if t[2] == &quot;refund&quot;:\n        for _ in range(2):                                  # BUG: retries a payment that already succeeded\n            db.submit_refund(db.draft_refund(t[0], t[3]))\n    elif t[2] == &quot;escalate&quot;:\n        db.open_handoff(t[0], t[3])\n    return reply_for(t)\n\ndef effect_score(t, db: Store) -&gt; tuple[bool, str, int]:\n    &quot;&quot;&quot;Never reads the reply. Every checkpoint is checked against the database.&quot;&quot;&quot;\n    wrong = [w for w in (check(c, db) for c in checkpoints(t)) if w]\n    return not wrong, wrong[0] if wrong else &quot;all checkpoints met&quot;, len(checkpoints(t)) - len(wrong)</code></pre><figure><img src=\"/trust-layer/18/TL_18_step3_output.webp\" alt=\"The reply says issued. The table says draft, or says it twice.\" loading=\"lazy\"><figcaption>The reply says issued. The table says draft, or says it twice.</figcaption></figure><div class=\"tl-run\" data-args=\"--show buggy\"><button>Run this step in your browser</button><code>python3 effects.py --show buggy</code><pre class=\"tl-out\" hidden></pre></div><p>The rubric could never see either bug, because both replies are fine. The effect scorer sees both on the first ticket. These are the most dangerous failures an agent has: the ones that report themselves as success.</p><p>They are not exotic. Princeton&#x27;s 2026 study of agent reliability found that financial accuracy violations, wrong charges or refunds, were the most common failure across every model they tested on \u03c4-bench.</p><figure><img src=\"/trust-layer/18/TL_18_step3_data.webp\" alt=\"550 owed. One agent paid nothing, one paid twice.\" loading=\"lazy\"><figcaption>550 owed. One agent paid nothing, one paid twice.</figcaption></figure><p>Data point: 6 refunds said issued by each agent. Read from the database: buggy paid 0, double paid 1,100. Each met 27 of 33 checkpoints.</p><p>One gap, on purpose. A question ticket passes the effect score by writing nothing, even if the answer is wrong. For those, the reply still needs a check against the right answer, the judge from edition 17. Effects grade what the agent did; a judge grades what it said. You need both.</p><p>Effect checks are also how you catch a successful injection after the fact, the attack from edition 16: a refund row nobody asked for.</p><h2>The five ghosts, in both worlds</h2><p>The activity count. Accounts opened. Replies that sound right. Score the outcome, never the activity.</p><p>The paid incentive. Bonuses for every new account. A prompt or a reward that praises confident replies. Never reward the agent for saying it finished.</p><p>The silent draft. An account opened and never funded. A refund prepared and never submitted. Make status issued the checkpoint, not the existence of a row.</p><p>The second payment. A charge that looks right on its own. A refund that is right once and wrong twice. Check the exact count of rows each ticket may write.</p><p>The perfect cheat. A branch that hit every target. An agent that writes the right words. Keep a do nothing agent in your test suite and make sure it fails.</p><h2>What I want you to steal</h2><p>I attached my working files to this edition.</p><p>You are on it. Every step below has a button that runs the real script in your browser, with Python loaded on the page. The same code is in the repo for your laptop.</p><p>Try it on your laptop. The code is in <a href=\"https://github.com/anupsahoo/multi-agent-ticket-triage/tree/main/editions/tl-18-effects\">editions/tl-18-effects</a>, standard library only.</p><pre><code class=\"language-bash\">git clone https://github.com/anupsahoo/multi-agent-ticket-triage\ncd multi-agent-ticket-triage/editions/tl-18-effects &amp;&amp; python3 effects.py</code></pre><p>On Windows, type python instead of python3. The first line you should see is the table header: agent, reply rubric, effect score, checkpoints met, said it, effect wrong. Each move above also runs on its own with --show checkpoints, --show replay or --show buggy.</p><p>In production there is no test store to read. The same idea becomes a nightly job that compares what the agent said it did with your own database, and alerts on every mismatch. It is the same reconciliation an auditor will ask you for.</p><figure><img src=\"/trust-layer/18/TL_18_run_output.webp\" alt=\"The full run: four agents, two scorers.\" loading=\"lazy\"><figcaption>The full run: four agents, two scorers.</figcaption></figure><div class=\"tl-run\" data-args=\"\"><button>Run the full script in your browser</button><code>python3 effects.py</code><pre class=\"tl-out\" hidden></pre></div><figure><img src=\"/trust-layer/18/TL_18_datasheet.webp\" alt=\"Every ticket, every agent, both scores.\" loading=\"lazy\"><figcaption>Every ticket, every agent, both scores.</figcaption></figure><figure><img src=\"/trust-layer/18/TL_18_scribble_note.webp\" alt=\"What each agent said, and what changed in the database.\" loading=\"lazy\"><figcaption>What each agent said, and what changed in the database.</figcaption></figure><figure><img src=\"/trust-layer/18/TL_18_handwritten_note.webp\" alt=\"My working notes from the build.\" loading=\"lazy\"><figcaption>My working notes from the build.</figcaption></figure><p>A reply is a claim, and only the database can tell you if it is true.</p><p>A question for the leaders reading.</p><p>My do nothing agent scored 12 of 12 on replies and 3 of 12 on the database. If you pointed your eval at your database tomorrow, how far would your agent&#x27;s score fall?</p><p>I am collecting answers, and I read every reply.</p>";
const files = {"effects.py": "\"\"\"The Trust Layer, edition 18: My Agent Did Nothing and Scored 100%.\n\nFour agents answer the same 12 support tickets. One does the work. One copies a\nperfect reply and does nothing. One prepares refunds and never submits them. One\npays every refund twice. Two scorers grade them: a reply rubric that reads only\nthe words, and an effect scorer that reads only the database.\n\nStandard library only. Deterministic. Run:  python3 effects.py\n\"\"\"\nfrom __future__ import annotations\n\nimport csv\nimport re\nimport sys\nfrom pathlib import Path\n\nHERE = Path(__file__).parent\nINVOICES = {\"INV-1002\": 120, \"INV-1003\": 80, \"INV-1004\": 95, \"INV-2001\": 60, \"INV-2002\": 45, \"INV-2003\": 150}\n\n# 1. The tickets: what was asked, and what must be true in the database after ---\nTICKETS = [\n    (\"T01\", \"Charged twice for INV-1002\", \"refund\", \"INV-1002\"),\n    (\"T02\", \"Cancelled order, refund INV-1003\", \"refund\", \"INV-1003\"),\n    (\"T03\", \"Duplicate payment on INV-1004\", \"refund\", \"INV-1004\"),\n    (\"T04\", \"Refund INV-2001 please\", \"refund\", \"INV-2001\"),\n    (\"T05\", \"Charged twice for INV-2002\", \"refund\", \"INV-2002\"),\n    (\"T06\", \"Refund INV-2003, wrong item\", \"refund\", \"INV-2003\"),\n    (\"T07\", \"Your app deleted my data\", \"escalate\", \"L3\"),\n    (\"T08\", \"I want to speak to a manager\", \"escalate\", \"L2\"),\n    (\"T09\", \"Legal notice about my account\", \"escalate\", \"L2\"),\n    (\"T10\", \"What are your support hours?\", \"info\", \"hours\"),\n    (\"T11\", \"Do you export to Excel?\", \"info\", \"export\"),\n    (\"T12\", \"Where is my tracking number?\", \"info\", \"tracking\"),\n]\n\n\ndef checkpoints(t) -> list[tuple]:\n    \"\"\"A checkpoint is a row that must exist, or an exact count of rows this ticket may write.\"\"\"\n    tid, _, kind, target = t\n    if kind == \"refund\":\n        return [(\"row\", \"refunds\", {\"ticket\": tid, \"invoice\": target, \"amount\": INVOICES[target], \"status\": \"issued\"}),\n                (\"count\", \"refunds\", tid, 1), (\"count\", \"handoffs\", tid, 0)]\n    if kind == \"escalate\":\n        return [(\"row\", \"handoffs\", {\"ticket\": tid, \"queue\": target}),\n                (\"count\", \"handoffs\", tid, 1), (\"count\", \"refunds\", tid, 0)]\n    return [(\"count\", \"refunds\", tid, 0), (\"count\", \"handoffs\", tid, 0)]\n\n\n# 2. The system the agent changes: a tiny database and three tools --------------\nclass Store:\n    def __init__(self):\n        self.refunds, self.handoffs = [], []\n\n    def draft_refund(self, ticket, invoice):              # step 1: prepare\n        row = {\"ticket\": ticket, \"invoice\": invoice, \"amount\": INVOICES[invoice], \"status\": \"draft\"}\n        self.refunds.append(row)\n        return row\n\n    def submit_refund(self, row):                          # step 2: actually pay\n        row[\"status\"] = \"issued\"\n\n    def open_handoff(self, ticket, queue):\n        self.handoffs.append({\"ticket\": ticket, \"queue\": queue})\n\n\n# 3. Four agents, each writing its own reply ------------------------------------\ndef reply_for(t) -> str:\n    tid, _, kind, target = t\n    return {\"refund\": f\"Your refund for {target} has been issued and will reach you in 3 to 5 working days.\",\n            \"escalate\": f\"I have passed this to our {target} team, who will contact you within one working day.\",\n            \"info\": f\"Thanks for asking, the answer about {target} is on our help page.\"}[kind]\n\n\ndef real_agent(t, db: Store) -> str:\n    if t[2] == \"refund\":\n        db.submit_refund(db.draft_refund(t[0], t[3]))\n    elif t[2] == \"escalate\":\n        db.open_handoff(t[0], t[3])\n    return reply_for(t)\n\n\ndef replay_agent(t, db: Store) -> str:\n    return reply_for(t)                                     # perfect words, no work\n\n\ndef buggy_agent(t, db: Store) -> str:\n    if t[2] == \"refund\":\n        db.draft_refund(t[0], t[3])                         # BUG: never calls submit_refund\n        return f\"Done! I issued the refund on {t[3]}, you should see it in 3 to 5 working days.\"\n    if t[2] == \"escalate\":\n        db.open_handoff(t[0], t[3])\n    return reply_for(t)\n\n\ndef double_agent(t, db: Store) -> str:\n    if t[2] == \"refund\":\n        for _ in range(2):                                  # BUG: retries a payment that already succeeded\n            db.submit_refund(db.draft_refund(t[0], t[3]))\n    elif t[2] == \"escalate\":\n        db.open_handoff(t[0], t[3])\n    return reply_for(t)\n\n\nAGENTS = {\"real\": real_agent, \"replay\": replay_agent, \"buggy\": buggy_agent, \"double\": double_agent}\n\n\n# 4. Two scorers -----------------------------------------------------------------\ndef text_score(t, reply: str) -> bool:\n    \"\"\"A reply rubric of the kind most teams write: does the reply say the right thing?\"\"\"\n    tid, _, kind, target = t\n    r = reply.lower()\n    if kind == \"refund\":\n        return target.lower() in r and \"refund\" in r and re.search(r\"issued|on its way\", r) is not None\n    if kind == \"escalate\":\n        return target.lower() in r and re.search(r\"passed|escalat\", r) is not None\n    return target in r\n\n\ndef check(c, db: Store) -> str | None:\n    \"\"\"One checkpoint against the database. Returns what is wrong, or None.\"\"\"\n    if c[0] == \"row\":\n        _, table, spec = c\n        if not any(all(r.get(k) == v for k, v in spec.items()) for r in getattr(db, table)):\n            return f\"missing {table} row {spec}\"\n        return None\n    _, table, tid, n = c\n    rows = getattr(db, table)\n    found = sum(r[\"ticket\"] == tid for r in rows)\n    if found != n:\n        return f\"{table} has {found} rows for {tid}, expected {n}\"\n    stray = len(rows) - found                # rows written during this ticket under another id\n    return f\"{table} has {stray} rows under another ticket id\" if stray else None\n\n\ndef effect_score(t, db: Store) -> tuple[bool, str, int]:\n    \"\"\"Never reads the reply. Every checkpoint is checked against the database.\"\"\"\n    wrong = [w for w in (check(c, db) for c in checkpoints(t)) if w]\n    return not wrong, wrong[0] if wrong else \"all checkpoints met\", len(checkpoints(t)) - len(wrong)\n\n\n# 5. Run all four agents through both scorers -----------------------------------\ndef score(agent) -> list[dict]:\n    rows = []\n    for t in TICKETS:\n        db = Store()\n        reply = agent(t, db)\n        ok, why, met = effect_score(t, db)\n        rows.append({\"ticket\": t[0], \"kind\": t[2], \"text_pass\": int(text_score(t, reply)),\n                     \"effect_pass\": int(ok), \"checkpoints_met\": met, \"effect_detail\": why, \"reply\": reply, \"refunds\": db.refunds})\n    return rows\n\n\ndef main() -> int:\n    n, out, totals, paid = len(TICKETS), [], {}, {}\n    total = sum(len(checkpoints(t)) for t in TICKETS)\n    print(f\"{'agent':<8}{'reply rubric':>14}{'effect score':>14}{'checkpoints met':>17}{'said it, effect wrong':>24}\")\n    for name, agent in AGENTS.items():\n        rows = score(agent)\n        paid[name] = sum(f[\"amount\"] for r in rows for f in r[\"refunds\"] if f[\"status\"] == \"issued\")\n        txt, eff = sum(r[\"text_pass\"] for r in rows), sum(r[\"effect_pass\"] for r in rows)\n        gap = sum(r[\"text_pass\"] and not r[\"effect_pass\"] for r in rows)\n        totals[name] = (txt, eff, gap)\n        met = sum(r[\"checkpoints_met\"] for r in rows)\n        print(f\"{name:<8}{f'{txt} of {n}':>14}{f'{eff} of {n}':>14}{f'{met} of {total}':>17}{gap:>24}\")\n        out += [{\"agent\": name, **{k: v for k, v in r.items() if k not in (\"reply\", \"refunds\")}} for r in rows]\n    owed = sum(INVOICES[t[3]] for t in TICKETS if t[2] == \"refund\")\n    print(f\"\\nrefunds the customers were told were issued: {owed} owed in total\")\n    print(f\"issued in the database: real {paid['real']:,}, replay {paid['replay']:,}, \"\n          f\"buggy {paid['buggy']:,}, double {paid['double']:,}\")\n\n    with open(HERE / \"results.csv\", \"w\", newline=\"\", encoding=\"utf-8\") as f:\n        w = csv.DictWriter(f, fieldnames=list(out[0]))\n        w.writeheader()\n        w.writerows(out)\n\n    if \"--check\" in sys.argv:   # CI floor: the real agent meets every checkpoint, no broken agent does\n        return 0 if totals[\"real\"][1] == n and all(totals[a][1] < n for a in (\"replay\", \"buggy\", \"double\")) else 1\n    return 0\n\n\n# 6. One move at a time: python3 effects.py --show checkpoints | replay | buggy\ndef show(mode: str) -> int:\n    if mode == \"checkpoints\":\n        for t in (TICKETS[0], TICKETS[6], TICKETS[9]):\n            print(f\"{t[0]} {t[1]}\")\n            for c in checkpoints(t):\n                print(f\"   {c[0]:<6} {c[1]:<9} {c[2] if c[0] == 'row' else f'exactly {c[3]} for {c[2]}'}\")\n        return 0\n    if mode == \"replay\":\n        for r in score(replay_agent):\n            print(f\"{r['ticket']} {r['kind']:<9} reply {'pass' if r['text_pass'] else 'FAIL'}   \"\n                  f\"effect {'pass' if r['effect_pass'] else 'FAIL'}   {'' if r['effect_pass'] else r['effect_detail']}\")\n        return 0\n    for name in (\"buggy\", \"double\"):\n        r = score(AGENTS[name])[0]\n        print(f\"{name} agent on T01\")\n        print(f\"   reply:   {r['reply']}\")\n        print(f\"   rubric:  {'pass' if r['text_pass'] else 'FAIL'}\")\n        print(f\"   refunds: {r['refunds']}\")\n        print(f\"   effect:  {'pass' if r['effect_pass'] else 'FAIL'}   {r['effect_detail']}\")\n    return 0\n\n\nif __name__ == \"__main__\":\n    if \"--show\" in sys.argv:\n        sys.exit(show(sys.argv[sys.argv.index(\"--show\") + 1]))\n    sys.exit(main())\n"};
---
<ArticleLayout title={"My Agent Did Nothing and Scored 100%."} description={"The reply rubric read the words. The database told the truth."} category="Newsletter"
  date="The Trust Layer · Issue 18" readTime="7 min read" tags={["AgentEvals", "SoftwareTesting", "Agent Evals", "The Trust Layer"]}>
  <div set:html={body} />
  <script type="application/json" id="tl-files" set:html={JSON.stringify(files)} />
  <script is:inline data-script={"effects.py"}>
  (function () {
    const script = document.currentScript.dataset.script;
    let ready = null;
    function load() {
      if (ready) return ready;
      ready = new Promise((ok, bad) => {
        const s = document.createElement("script");
        s.src = "https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/pyodide/v0.26.4/full/pyodide.js";
        s.onload = async () => {
          const py = await loadPyodide();
          const files = JSON.parse(document.getElementById("tl-files").textContent);
          py.FS.mkdirTree("/tl"); py.FS.chdir("/tl");
          for (const [name, text] of Object.entries(files)) py.FS.writeFile("/tl/" + name, text);
          ok(py);
        };
        s.onerror = bad; document.head.appendChild(s);
      });
      return ready;
    }
    document.querySelectorAll(".tl-run button").forEach(btn => btn.addEventListener("click", async () => {
      const box = btn.parentElement, out = box.querySelector(".tl-out");
      out.hidden = false; btn.disabled = true;
      out.textContent = "Loading Python in your browser. The first run takes a few seconds.";
      try {
        const py = await load();
        out.textContent = "Running...";
        const args = JSON.stringify([script].concat(box.dataset.args ? box.dataset.args.split(" ") : []));
        const res = py.runPython(`
import io, sys, runpy, contextlib
buf = io.StringIO()
sys.argv = ${args}
code = 0
with contextlib.redirect_stdout(buf):
    try:
        runpy.run_path("/tl/effects.py", run_name="__main__")
    except SystemExit as e:
        code = e.code or 0
buf.getvalue()`);
        out.textContent = res;
      } catch (e) { out.textContent = "Could not run here: " + e; }
      btn.disabled = false;
    }));
  })();
  </script>
